# Writes & transactions

<p class="lede">
  Two layers, one pipeline: typed Kysely-style mutation builders on top, column-based batches
  underneath. Every write validates before it stores anything, publishes immutable segments, and
  reports its own metrics. Published blocks are never updated in place.
</p>

## Typed writes

The [query builder](/docs/queries/)'s facade carries the write side too, with Kysely conventions:
`execute()` resolves to an array (one result object, or the `returning(...)` rows), and the
idiomatic single-result call is `executeTakeFirst()` / `executeTakeFirstOrThrow()`.

```ts
const { numInsertedRows } = await db
  .insertInto("people")
  .values([{ name: "Ada", score: 10 }]) // omitted nullable columns pad with null
  .executeTakeFirstOrThrow();

// returning() rewrites the result type to the projected rows.
const written = await db
  .insertInto("people")
  .values({ name: "Grace", score: 20 })
  .returningAll()
  .executeTakeFirstOrThrow(); // { name: "Grace", score: 20, joined: null }

await db.insertInto("people").values({ name: "Ada", score: 99 }).orReplace().execute(); // upsert

const bumped = await db
  .updateTable("people")
  .set((eb) => ({ score: eb("score", "+", 1) })) // or set("score", 5)
  .where("joined", "is", null)
  .returning(["name", "score"]) // post-update values
  .execute();

const removed = await db
  .deleteFrom("people")
  .where("score", "<", 10)
  .returningAll() // the deleted rows
  .execute();
```

- `set({ ...patch })` skips `undefined` entries, so spread-patches never accidentally null a
  column; explicit `null` still writes NULL. Literals validate eagerly.
- Insert `returning` echoes the written rows — exact, because the engine has no defaults or
  generated columns. Update/delete `returning` rows come from the statement's own snapshot:
  post-update values for updates, the rows as read for deletes.
- Updates and deletes compile to the same mutation statements SQL parses into and run through the
  engine's read-keys-then-apply pipeline; a competing writer fails the statement explicitly rather
  than interleaving.
- Result counts are plain numbers (`numInsertedRows: number`), not bigints.

Everything below is the batch layer the builders compile onto — use it directly for bulk ingestion
or when compile-time types are not needed.

## Creating tables

A table uses only the four logical types. It may name one non-null column as a unique key, and
columns may be declared `nullable`.

```ts
await database.createTable({
  name: "people",
  uniqueKey: "name",
  columns: [
    { name: "name", type: "string" },
    { name: "score", type: "number" },
    { name: "joined", type: "datetime", nullable: true },
  ],
});
```

## Batch writes

Inserts validate column names, row counts, null values, and value types before writing.
`upsertBatch` updates rows matching the unique key and inserts new keys. `updateBatch` writes
immutable patch segments containing only the key and changed columns; it rejects missing keys and
rechecks them after conflicts. Rows are deleted by unique key.

```ts
const result = await database.insertBatch("people", {
  columns: {
    name: ["Ada", "Grace"],
    score: [10, 20],
    joined: [new Date(), null],
  },
});

await database.upsertBatch("people", {
  columns: {
    name: ["Grace", "Katherine"],
    score: [25, 30],
    joined: [new Date(), new Date()],
  },
});

await database.updateBatch("people", {
  keys: ["Grace", "Katherine"],
  changes: { score: [26, 31] },
});

await database.deleteBatch("people", { keys: ["Ada"] });
```

Unique-key checks use atomic, versioned persistent key chunks, avoiding both table-block scans and
one-IndexedDB-operation-per-key overhead. The write result reports row count, block count, saved
bytes, database version, encoding/staging/commit time, retries, rows per second, and write
amplification.

## Reading tables

`readTable` accepts a column projection and an optional historical version. It fetches the required
blocks for each visible segment in bulk windows of up to 16 block IDs; replay may additionally load
a unique-key column needed to apply mutations.

```ts
const scores = await database.readTable("people", {
  columns: ["name", "score"],
});
```

## Buffered writer

A buffered writer combines individual rows into column batches and flushes at a row, byte, or age
limit.

```ts
const writer = database.bufferedWriter("people", {
  mode: "upsert",
  maxRows: 1_000,
  maxBytes: 1024 * 1024,
  maxAgeMs: 1_000,
});
await writer.add({ name: "Linus", score: 30, joined: new Date() });

// Called by a worker-side handler when a main-thread lifecycle proxy requests a flush.
writer.requestFlush();
await writer.close();
```

An age-triggered flush waits behind an already-running batch and then drains rows accepted during
that batch, so the timer cannot be consumed while data remains pending. `requestFlush()` is
non-blocking and reports failures through the writer's `onError`. A main-thread worker proxy can
use `attachLifecycleFlush()` to send that request when the document becomes hidden or the page
fires `pagehide`.

> Lifecycle flushing is an optimization, not an unload-time durability guarantee. The durability
> contract ends at a successfully committed IndexedDB transaction; browser shutdown cannot be made
> reliable by visibility or page-lifecycle signals.

## Maintenance

Compaction, garbage collection, and spill cleanup are stepped, durable, and cancellable. Each
records a revisioned job that survives yields and database reopens.

```ts
// Advance a durable physical rewrite by one output block.
let progress = await database.compactTableStep("events", { maxBlocks: 1 });
while (progress.result === null) {
  if (progress.jobId === null) throw new Error("Expected a compaction job");
  progress = await database.resumeCompactionJob(progress.jobId, { maxBlocks: 1 });
}
const jobs = await database.listCompactionJobs("events");

// Reclaim unreachable history and terminal-job artifacts to completion.
const collection = await database.collectGarbage({
  maxItemsPerStep: 64,
  maxPlanningItems: 1024,
});

// Or drive the same durable pass explicitly.
let gcProgress = await database.collectGarbageStep({ maxItems: 8 });
while (gcProgress.result === null) {
  gcProgress = await database.resumeGarbageCollectionJob(gcProgress.jobId, { maxItems: 8 });
}

// Reclaim query spill pages abandoned by an abrupt tab loss once owner leases expire.
const spillCleanup = await database.cleanupQuerySpill({ maxOwners: 64 });
```

- `compactTable()` drives the checkpointed rewrite to completion; `compactTableStep()` advances it
  by at most `maxBlocks` output blocks; `resumeCompactionJob()` continues a known job after a yield
  or reopen.
- `cancelCompactionJob(jobId)` durably settles an unpublished job as cancelled and atomically
  aborts its linked transaction. It is idempotent and returns the terminal state plus any published
  version, so a caller sees when publication had already won.
- `collectGarbage()` / `collectGarbageStep()` run a lease-aware, restart-safe reachability pass.
  Roots include the current manifest, unexpired reader/backup leases, active transactions, and
  active compaction sources; historical manifests are tombstoned rather than deleted.
- `cleanupQuerySpill()` reclaims spill pages whose owner lease is expired or missing; a
  concurrently renewed owner is retained rather than torn down mid-query.

> Cancellation is cooperative: an in-flight step observes it at its next durable boundary. Blocks
> already written by a cancelled job stay unreachable until a later garbage-collection pass
> revalidates and reclaims them.

See [Schema & migrations](/docs/schema/) for the typed alternative to `createTable`.

# Workers & multi-tab

<p class="lede">
  The library ships both sides of the worker channel: a ready-made worker entry
  (`@minnowdb/core/worker`) and a main-thread client (`@minnowdb/core/client`). Your application
  contributes exactly one line — constructing the `Worker` — because that is the only line your
  bundler must be able to see.
</p>

## Quick start

```ts
import { MinnowDatabaseClient } from "@minnowdb/core/client";
import { Minnow, column, schema, table, type InferDatabase } from "@minnowdb/core";

const people = table("people", {
  name: column.string().unique(),
  score: column.number(),
});
const appSchema = schema([people]);

const client = new MinnowDatabaseClient(
  new Worker(new URL("@minnowdb/core/worker", import.meta.url), { type: "module" }),
  { store: { kind: "indexeddb", name: "app-db" } },
);
await client.migrate(appSchema);

// The typed facade wraps the client exactly as it wraps an in-worker database.
const db = new Minnow<InferDatabase<typeof appSchema>>(client, { schema: appSchema });
await db
  .insertInto("people")
  .values([
    { name: "Ada", score: 10 },
    { name: "Grace", score: 20 },
  ])
  .execute();
const rows = await db
  .selectFrom("people")
  .select(["name", "score"])
  .orderBy("score", "desc")
  .execute(); // Array<{ name: string; score: number }>
```

The client sends its configuration to the worker in an init frame, so the stock entry needs no
configuration of its own. Because the channel is ordered you may issue calls immediately;
`await client.ready()` exists when you want to surface store-open failures eagerly. IndexedDB
access, block decoding, planning, and query execution all run in the worker; the main thread holds
only this proxy. The client also exposes the raw layer directly — `client.query(sql)`,
`client.insertBatch(...)`, `client.createTable(...)` — mirroring the full database API.

## Why you construct the Worker

A worker needs a script URL at runtime, and
`new Worker(new URL("…", import.meta.url), { type: "module" })` is only rewritten correctly when
your bundler sees that exact expression in your build graph. Buried inside a published library it
degrades differently under every bundler — which is why the one bundler-sensitive line lives in
your code. It also keeps lifecycle ownership honest: you decide when the worker is created, shared,
and terminated, and content-security policies (`worker-src`) are satisfied by your origin rather
than worked around with blob URLs.

## What changes across the boundary

- Every member is asynchronous. Members that are synchronous in the worker
  (`PreparedQuery.execute()`, stats getters, `close()`) return promises on the client, and getter
  pairs become methods — `writer.stats()`, `prepared.memoryUsage()`.
- `prepareQuery()` still holds its materialized snapshot inputs in the worker until `close()`, but
  a client-side `execute()` measures execution plus one channel round trip — benchmark inside the
  worker when isolating executor time.
- `migrate()` accepts the same schema DSL (serialized over the wire automatically) and reports its
  steps in a data-only wire form.
- Typed errors are rehydrated on the client: `instanceof UniqueConstraintError` works and carries
  its fields; stack traces point into the worker.
- Construction options that are functions (`now`, `createId`, a custom store) cannot cross the
  channel — use a custom entry (below).

The typed facade is unaffected by all of this: compiled plans and statements cross the channel by
structured clone, so `selectFrom`, the typed [writes](/docs/writes/) with `returning`, and
`.live()` behave identically on the main thread.

## Buffered writers and live queries

Stateful handles proxy transparently: the writer's age timer runs on the worker's clock, and
live-query callbacks arrive as events. Cross-tab commit hints work by naming a BroadcastChannel the
worker should join.

```ts
const writer = client.bufferedWriter("people", {
  maxRows: 500,
  onError: (error) => console.error("background flush failed", error),
});
await writer.add({ name: "Edsger", score: 30 });
await writer.close();

const live = client.liveQueries({ channelName: "app-db-commits" });
const subscription = await live.subscribe("SELECT name, score FROM people ORDER BY score DESC", {
  onChange: (result) => render(result.rows),
});
// … later
await subscription.close();
await live.close();
await client.close({ terminateWorker: true });
```

## Bundler setups

**Vite and webpack 5** both understand the quick-start pattern as written — they see the
`new URL("@minnowdb/core/worker", import.meta.url)` expression, resolve the package subpath, and
emit a separate worker chunk. Nothing else is required.

**esbuild** (and Parcel 2 with default settings) does not rewrite `new URL` worker expressions.
Build the worker entry as its own bundle and point the Worker at the emitted file:

```ts
// worker.ts — your one-line worker entry, bundled separately:
import "@minnowdb/core/worker";

// esbuild app.ts worker.ts --bundle --format=esm --outdir=dist --splitting

// app.ts:
const client = new MinnowDatabaseClient(
  new Worker(new URL("./worker.js", import.meta.url), { type: "module" }),
);
```

**No bundler** — module workers cannot resolve bare specifiers (import maps do not apply inside
workers in all engines yet), so use full URLs from your chosen CDN or vendored files:

```ts
// db-worker.js — served from your origin:
import "https://cdn.example.com/@minnowdb/core/dist/worker.js";

// main page:
import { MinnowDatabaseClient } from "https://cdn.example.com/@minnowdb/core/dist/client.js";
const client = new MinnowDatabaseClient(
  new Worker(new URL("./db-worker.js", import.meta.url), { type: "module" }),
);
```

## Custom worker entries

The stock entry covers the cloneable configuration surface: the store descriptor (`indexeddb` or
`memory`) plus `compression`, `rowsPerBlock`, `maxCommitRetries`, `spillOwnerLeaseMs`, and
`prepareCacheBytes`. When construction needs things a message cannot carry — a custom `BlockStore`,
deterministic `now`/`createId` seams — write your own entry and expose the database you built:

```ts
// my-worker.ts
import { MinnowDatabase, exposeDatabase } from "@minnowdb/core";
import { IndexedDbBlockStore } from "@minnowdb/core/storage";

const store = await IndexedDbBlockStore.open({ name: "app-db", durability: "strict" });
exposeDatabase(new MinnowDatabase(store, { compression: "gzip" }), self, {
  onDispose: () => store.close(),
});
```

`exposeDatabase()` answers the same client frames, acknowledging the client's init without
reconstructing anything, so the main-thread code is unchanged. Underneath both entries sits
`@minnowdb/core/worker-protocol`: versioned, structured-clone-safe RPC frames with method
whitelists per handle — the worker never dispatches arbitrary property access.

> The worker changes where work runs, not the durability contract: correctness never depends on
> BroadcastChannel, Web Locks, page-close handlers, or successful background work. IndexedDB is
> authoritative, and the contract still ends at a successfully committed IndexedDB transaction.
